Instruction: Read the segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.\nQuestion: Is the 'ability to consummate' concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts?\nOption A: No\nOption B: Yes\n\nMerger Agreement: 'Company Material Adverse Effect' means any effect, change, event, circumstance or development that, individually or in the aggregate, has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on (i) the business, financial condition, or results of operations of the Company and its Subsidiaries, taken as a whole, or (ii) the ability of the Company to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement; provided, however, that, in the case of clause (i), the following shall not be deemed to constitute a Company Material Adverse Effect: (a) changes in general economic or political conditions or conditions in the markets in which the Company operates, (b) changes in laws, regulations, or accounting principles, (c) acts of war, terrorism, or natural disasters, (d) any failure by the Company to meet internal or published projections, forecasts, or revenue or earnings predictions, (e) the announcement or pendency of this Agreement or the transactions contemplated hereby, including the impact thereof on relationships, contractual or otherwise, with customers, suppliers, or employees, except that this clause (e) shall not apply to any representation or warranty set forth in Section 3.4(b), and (f) any decline in the market price or trading volume of the Company's stock, in and of itself, provided that any underlying cause of such decline may be considered. Furthermore, any effect, change, event, or circumstance described in clauses (a), (b), or (c) shall be taken into account in determining whether a Company Material Adverse Effect has occurred to the extent that such effect has a disproportionate adverse effect on the Company relative to other companies in the industries in which the Company operates. (Pages 12-13)\nAnswer:
